#4 - Equipment Inventory via Google Sheets

What & Why

The FES Center plans to maintain an equipment inventory in a Google Sheet (or Excel file shared via Google Sheets). The Inventory screen in the app should dynamically fetch and display that sheet's data without requiring a backend database. This lets non-technical staff update the inventory just by editing the spreadsheet — no app release needed.

Done looks like

The Equipment Inventory screen fetches live data from a configured Google Sheet
The first row of the sheet is treated as column headers; subsequent rows are inventory items
Items are displayed as clean cards or a scrollable table — layout adapts to whatever columns exist in the sheet (flexible, not hardcoded to specific column names)
A search/filter bar at the top lets investigators filter items by keyword across all fields
Pull-to-refresh reloads from the sheet; a loading state and error state are shown appropriately
The Sheet ID and Sheet name are configurable via environment variable, not hardcoded — so the sheet can be renamed or replaced without a code change
If the sheet contains an "Image" column with image URLs, those images are shown in the card; if not, a placeholder icon is shown (graceful degradation)
Out of scope

Editing inventory items from the app (sheet is read-only from the app's perspective)
Authentication / private sheets (the sheet must be "Anyone with the link can view")
Offline support / caching beyond React Query's default cache TTL
Category filtering UI (can be added later once column structure is decided)
Notes

Use the Google Sheets API v4 endpoint: https://sheets.googleapis.com/v4/spreadsheets/{SHEET_ID}/values/{SHEET_NAME}?valueRenderOption=FORMATTED_VALUE&key={API_KEY}
The Google Sheets API key must be a server-side secret GOOGLE_SHEETS_API_KEY in the api-server — never exposed to the Expo client. The api-server proxies the request.
INVENTORY_SHEET_ID and INVENTORY_SHEET_NAME (defaults to Sheet1) should be environment variables on the api-server.
The sheet must be shared as "Anyone on the internet with this link — Viewer" for the API key to work without OAuth.
When the inventory is first set up, the team should decide columns. Recommended starter columns: Name, Category, Location, Serial Number, Status, Notes, Image (URL). The app handles any column set dynamically.
Steps

API proxy route — Add a /api/inventory route in the api-server. It reads GOOGLE_SHEETS_API_KEY, INVENTORY_SHEET_ID, INVENTORY_SHEET_NAME from env, fetches the sheet values, parses row 0 as headers and rows 1+ as data objects, and returns { headers: string[], items: Record<string, string>[] }.
Inventory screen — Replace the stub app/equipment-inventory.tsx. Use @tanstack/react-query to fetch /api/inventory. Render a FlatList of item cards. Each card is built dynamically from the headers returned by the API — no hardcoded field names.
Search filter — Add a TextInput search bar above the list. Filter items client-side (from the cached query data) by matching the search term against all field values. This avoids extra API calls for filtering.
Image handling — If an "Image" header exists in the sheet and a row has a non-empty value for it, render it as a thumbnail in the card using expo-image. Otherwise show a package/box icon placeholder.
Empty & error states — "No inventory items found" when the sheet is empty or search yields nothing. "Could not load inventory" with retry button on fetch error. Log a clear server-side warning if env vars are missing.
Environment documentation — Add GOOGLE_SHEETS_API_KEY, INVENTORY_SHEET_ID, INVENTORY_SHEET_NAME to the project documentation.
